ot allowed but he  realized that if he could make an  argument for a new device being used  primarily for cooling then they could  use a fan that sucked air from the  bottom of the car and run it through the  engine the energy from the system would  primarily be used to cool the engine but  it had the added bonus of sucking the  carrot onto the road  the bravo bt 46 from the Lotus pipe 79  faced off in the 1978 swedish grand prix  and despite complaints from colin  chapman the founder of lotus the fan car  still ran Mario Andretti driving the  Lotus took an early lead but the Bravo  driven by Niki Lauda was gradually  gaining and eventually overtook and  riding on the outside Niki Lauda and the  brown bt 46 went on to win the race by  34 seconds but this would be the fan  carrots first and final competitive race  other drivers complained that the car  was firing rocks and dust at the back  and despite the carribean was in the  regulation the other teams pressured the  FIA to outlaw the car problem we're told  they could run the car for the rest of  the season but instead decided to  withdraw leaving the door open for Lotus  to win the 1978 Formula One season the  following year loaded slipped fourth  place as other teams caught up for  ground effect technology  I think this exemplifies why I enjoy  racing for me it's less about the  drivers and more about the engineers  behind them competing to create the best  vehicle possible within the rules today  engineers have a huge amount of tools at  their disposal to rapidly prototype new  car bodies I mentioned that the type 78  was tested in a winter and that testing  helped towards the discovery of ground  effects but prototypes are  time-consuming to make and winter homes  aren't always available to everyone one  of the biggest developments in f1 and  engineering in general the past two  decades has been the advancement of  computer-aided engineering with this  method we can simply generate a huge  variety of models an